#!/bin/sh
# mux-conf_lib - the ONE comment/normalize rule for mux's line-directive config
# (layouts, themes, agents). Sourced (functions only); every config PARSE site
# runs its input through mux_conf_clean, so the rule lives in exactly one place
# and cannot drift between layouts, themes, and agent profiles.
#
# mux_conf_clean: stdin -> clean directive lines. In order it
#   - drops FULL-LINE comments (optional indent, then #),
#   - strips INLINE comments: WHITESPACE then # to end of line. A hash that
#     BEGINS a comment goes; a hash INSIDE a token stays, so `bg=#3f5f00` (no
#     space before #) survives while `theme slate  # note` loses the note. The
#     one casualty is a literal " #" inside a pane/bottom COMMAND (rare: use
#     `pane sh -c '...'`, or avoid the space-hash),
#   - trims trailing whitespace (so `theme slate ` is not the name "slate "),
#     WHICH IS ALSO WHAT HANDLES A CRLF FILE saved by another editor. There
#     used to be an explicit `s/\r$//` first, and it was redundant: POSIX
#     `[:space:]` includes carriage return, so this rule already strips a
#     trailing CR. Measured before removing it rather than argued: 4,680
#     single-line inputs over the characters these rules care about, zero
#     difference with and without, and every rule here is per-line (no `N`,
#     no hold space) so single-line equivalence is total. It was also the one
#     rule no mutation could kill, since dropping it changed nothing, which
#     is this package's standing signal that a guard is redundant rather
#     than untested. `test/mux-conf.t`'s `crlf` case still asserts the
#     behaviour, and would catch a platform whose [:space:] disagreed,
#   - drops the blank lines all of the above leaves behind.
# ONE EXPRESSION PER LINE, IN A VARIABLE, rather than five `-e` flags on a
# continued command, and that is a TESTABILITY decision rather than a style
# one. Written as a continuation every rule but the last ended in a `\`, and
# a corpus anchor cannot be a line continuation, so four of the five rules
# here could not be individually killed: this lib has 21 consumers and had
# zero mutation records, which is the gap the consumers' tests hide.
#
# Same answer 0.72 reached for latch's `untrusted` reason and 2026-10-05 for
# the bottom split: when a guard cannot be expressed as a record, that is a
# fact about the code's SHAPE. sed takes a newline-separated script, so the
# five lines below are the five rules, each its own anchor, with no extra
# process and no behaviour change (verified against the old form over the
# whole of test/mux-conf.t's input set).
#
# The lines sit at column 0 because they are sed's script, not shell: leading
# whitespace inside this string would be data sed reads as part of a command.
mux_conf_clean() {
  _cc_script='/^[[:space:]]*#/d
s/[[:space:]]#.*$//
s/[[:space:]]*$//
/^[[:space:]]*$/d'
  sed "$_cc_script"
}
